The handle gives all at once. Your boot skids on the frosted deck, and your shoulder meets the door frame hard enough to rattle your teeth. Pain runs down to the elbow. The handle lies in your hand, bolts snapped. The door has not moved.

Behind it, a long breath. "That sounded expensive," the voice says. "Is the compass still pointing?"

