You hold a mug of reconstituted barley tea in both hands, and the steam smells faintly of the ship's coolant loop. Across the galley table, the brass star-compass lies beside your plate of crackers and rehydrated cheese, its needle swung stubbornly toward the cold locker door. Somewhere aft, the engineer is arguing with a pipe, and you can hear the pipe winning. The navigator, by the sound of it, is still teaching the loader drone to say good morning, and the drone has settled on a greeting that is mostly a long, hopeful whirr. You turn the compass a quarter turn with one finger, and the needle drifts back to the locker, patient as a cat at a closed door. From behind the frost-rimed steel comes a thin, tired voice. <npc>"I can hear the tea, you know. Some of us have been in here a while."</npc>

